{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794529171518,"sessionID":"ses_e2dbc36c0ffexACT5imzdktU7L","part":{"id":"prt_1d246183e001NgsCVWoH5VFkPe","sessionID":"ses_e2dbc36c0ffexACT5imzdktU7L","messageID":"msg_1d2461312001TQLAL0e3z5066B","type":"step-start","snapshot":"b140a0836d01dfef1e41ea26905db8487c4eda39"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794529183311,"sessionID":"ses_e2dbc36c0ffexACT5imzdktU7L","part":{"id":"prt_1d2463397002qptV1U21HtF4Vt","sessionID":"ses_e2dbc36c0ffexACT5imzdktU7L","messageID":"msg_1d2461312001TQLAL0e3z5066B","type":"text","text":"Now I'll write the contract description into the doc.","time":{"start":1794529178519,"end":1794529183311}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794529183426,"sessionID":"ses_e2dbc36c0ffexACT5imzdktU7L","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2463434001tkVnE0w21wyn8X","sessionID":"ses_e2dbc36c0ffexACT5imzdktU7L","messageID":"msg_1d2461312001TQLAL0e3z5066B","type":"tool","id":"call_67fbc337020041bf8fc5cbff","tool":"edit","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md","oldString":"A hub also leaves `command` off when the same command already ranks in the result through its own command row. `enrichCompiledRoutes()` reads each row's slash string from `BRIDGE_COMMAND_BY_ID`, so one command never takes two slots.\n\n---","newString":"A hub also leaves `command` off when the same command already ranks in the result through its own command row. `enrichCompiledRoutes()` takes the `command` fields present in the result as the commands already offered, so one command never takes two slots.\n\n### Recommendation Contract (`kind`, `command`, `policy`, `ownerSkillId`)\n\nEach recommendation carries up to four optional fields that let a reader act on it without a second lookup. `kind` is `'skill'` or `'command'` and says which face the pick is, so a caller can tell a skill entry from a command row that scored as a candidate. `command` is the runnable slash string, such as `/speckit:save`. `policy` is `'run'`, `'start'` or `'ask'` and says how a caller may run that command. `ownerSkillId` names the skill that owns the command, which is the grouping key when several commands belong to one hub. All four stay optional in `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` so a response from a runtime that predates them still parses.\n\n`lib/command-attach.ts` is the single source for the values. Its generated `COMMAND_ROWS` block maps each id a scorer can return, whether the TypeScript id, the Python id or the entry id, to `{ command, policy?, ownerSkillId }`, and `commandRowFor(id)` reads it with an own-key check so an inherited name such as `constructor` never resolves to a row. `publicRecommendation()` in `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` looks one up for the recommendation's own id and copies its `command`, its `policy` when present and its `ownerSkillId`, and it forwards the scorer's `kind`. An id with no row adds none of the three. A command attached through the compiled route takes its `policy` from `COMMAND_POLICIES[command]` because that generated table is keyed by runnable string, and it takes no `ownerSkillId` because the recommendation already names the hub.\n\nThe renderer and the degraded path read the same contract. `lib/render.ts` builds the brief label `<ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)` from the recommendation's own fields first and falls back to the generated row for anything omitted, so a hook that predates the fields still prints a runnable command. The CLI degraded path in `skill-advisor-cli.ts` emits a `kind: 'command'` entry with all four fields when the prompt contains the row's bare slash command, and otherwise emits the row's `ownerSkillId` as a `kind: 'skill'` entry so the fallback brief names a real skill instead of a raw row id.\n\n---"},"output":"Edited .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md (1 replacement)","title":"edit","metadata":{"metadata":{"files":[{"file":".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md","patch":"Index: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md\n===================================================================\n--- .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md\n+++ .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md\n@@ -35,10 +35,18 @@\n The request option `options.includeCompiledRoute` is an optional boolean that defaults to true. When a caller passes `false` the handler skips `enrichCompiledRoutes()` on both the fresh and the cached return (`handlers/advisor-recommend.ts:563-565`, `632-634`), so no `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess starts and no recommendation carries `compiledRoute` or `command`. The prompt hooks pass `true` (`hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts:238`), because the `command` field is derived from the compiled route. The OpenCode plugin does not pass the option and keeps receiving `compiledRoute`. The option is declared in the zod schema (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:287`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:23`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:57`).\n \n The request option `options.compiledRouteTopOnly` is an optional boolean that defaults to false. When a caller passes `true`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` runs the route lookup for the first recommendation only and returns every later one unchanged, so only the top pick can carry `compiledRoute` or `command`. Each lookup is a `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess, so this saves one subprocess for each further eligible hub. The prompt hooks pass `true` with `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the brief leads with the top pick. The option is declared in the zod schema (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:290`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:24`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:58`).\n \n-A hub also leaves `command` off when the same command already ranks in the result through its own command row. `enrichCompiledRoutes()` reads each row's slash string from `BRIDGE_COMMAND_BY_ID`, so one command never takes two slots.\n+A hub also leaves `command` off when the same command already ranks in the result through its own command row. `enrichCompiledRoutes()` takes the `command` fields present in the result as the commands already offered, so one command never takes two slots.\n \n+### Recommendation Contract (`kind`, `command`, `policy`, `ownerSkillId`)\n+\n+Each recommendation carries up to four optional fields that let a reader act on it without a second lookup. `kind` is `'skill'` or `'command'` and says which face the pick is, so a caller can tell a skill entry from a command row that scored as a candidate. `command` is the runnable slash string, such as `/speckit:save`. `policy` is `'run'`, `'start'` or `'ask'` and says how a caller may run that command. `ownerSkillId` names the skill that owns the command, which is the grouping key when several commands belong to one hub. All four stay optional in `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` so a response from a runtime that predates them still parses.\n+\n+`lib/command-attach.ts` is the single source for the values. Its generated `COMMAND_ROWS` block maps each id a scorer can return, whether the TypeScript id, the Python id or the entry id, to `{ command, policy?, ownerSkillId }`, and `commandRowFor(id)` reads it with an own-key check so an inherited name such as `constructor` never resolves to a row. `publicRecommendation()` in `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` looks one up for the recommendation's own id and copies its `command`, its `policy` when present and its `ownerSkillId`, and it forwards the scorer's `kind`. An id with no row adds none of the three. A command attached through the compiled route takes its `policy` from `COMMAND_POLICIES[command]` because that generated table is keyed by runnable string, and it takes no `ownerSkillId` because the recommendation already names the hub.\n+\n+The renderer and the degraded path read the same contract. `lib/render.ts` builds the brief label `<ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)` from the recommendation's own fields first and falls back to the generated row for anything omitted, so a hook that predates the fields still prints a runnable command. The CLI degraded path in `skill-advisor-cli.ts` emits a `kind: 'command'` entry with all four fields when the prompt contains the row's bare slash command, and otherwise emits the row's `ownerSkillId` as a `kind: 'skill'` entry so the fallback brief names a real skill instead of a raw row id.\n+\n ---\n \n ## 3. SOURCE FILES\n \n","status":"modified","additions":9,"deletions":1}],"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Edited .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md (1 replacement)"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md\",\"oldString\":\"A hub also leaves `command` off when the same command already ranks in the result through its own command row. `enrichCompiledRoutes()` reads each row's slash string from `BRIDGE_COMMAND_BY_ID`, so one command never takes two slots.\\n\\n---\",\"newString\":\"A hub also leaves `command` off when the same command already ranks in the result through its own command row. `enrichCompiledRoutes()` takes the `command` fields present in the result as the commands already offered, so one command never takes two slots.\\n\\n### Recommendation Contract (`kind`, `command`, `policy`, `ownerSkillId`)\\n\\nEach recommendation carries up to four optional fields that let a reader act on it without a second lookup. `kind` is `'skill'` or `'command'` and says which face the pick is, so a caller can tell a skill entry from a command row that scored as a candidate. `command` is the runnable slash string, such as `/speckit:save`. `policy` is `'run'`, `'start'` or `'ask'` and says how a caller may run that command. `ownerSkillId` names the skill that owns the command, which is the grouping key when several commands belong to one hub. All four stay optional in `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` so a response from a runtime that predates them still parses.\\n\\n`lib/command-attach.ts` is the single source for the values. Its generated `COMMAND_ROWS` block maps each id a scorer can return, whether the TypeScript id, the Python id or the entry id, to `{ command, policy?, ownerSkillId }`, and `commandRowFor(id)` reads it with an own-key check so an inherited name such as `constructor` never resolves to a row. `publicRecommendation()` in `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` looks one up for the recommendation's own id and copies its `command`, its `policy` when present and its `ownerSkillId`, and it forwards the scorer's `kind`. An id with no row adds none of the three. A command attached through the compiled route takes its `policy` from `COMMAND_POLICIES[command]` because that generated table is keyed by runnable string, and it takes no `ownerSkillId` because the recommendation already names the hub.\\n\\nThe renderer and the degraded path read the same contract. `lib/render.ts` builds the brief label `<ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)` from the recommendation's own fields first and falls back to the generated row for anything omitted, so a hook that predates the fields still prints a runnable command. The CLI degraded path in `skill-advisor-cli.ts` emits a `kind: 'command'` entry with all four fields when the prompt contains the row's bare slash command, and otherwise emits the row's `ownerSkillId` as a `kind: 'skill'` entry so the fallback brief names a real skill instead of a raw row id.\\n\\n---\"}"},"time":{"start":1794529178676,"end":1794529183426}}}}
